@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')
  Resources
@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>A resource connects a database table to the panel. The panel lists, creates, edits and deletes its rows. A resource is a Rust type that implements the <code>IgniteResource</code> trait. You can write the impl by hand, or let <code>#[derive(IgniteResource)]</code> write it from a Diesel model.</p>

  <h2>Address and name</h2>
  <p>Three constants name the resource:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>SLUG</code> is the URL segment. A slug of <code>articles</code> mounts the resource at <code>/admin/articles</code>. Two resources cannot share a slug.</li>
    <li><code>TITLE</code> is the heading and the navigation label, such as <code>Articles</code>.</li>
    <li><code>PER_PAGE</code> is the number of rows on a list page. It is 20 unless you change it, as in <code>const PER_PAGE: i64 = 50;</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The derive takes the slug from the table name, and the title from the table name with a capital letter, unless you set <code>title</code>.</p>

  <h2>Derive a resource</h2>
  <p>The derive reads a Diesel model. The model also derives <code>Queryable</code>, <code>Selectable</code> and <code>Serialize</code>, because the derive does not write them. This model has a <code>title</code> that is searchable and sortable, and a <code>body</code> that is a multi-line field and hidden from the list:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_core::IgniteResource;
use serde::Serialize;

use crate::schema;

#[derive(diesel::Queryable, diesel::Selectable, Serialize, IgniteResource)]
#[diesel(table_name = schema::articles)]
#[ignite(table = schema::articles, title = "Articles")]
pub struct Article {
    pub id: i64,
    #[ignite(searchable, sortable)]
    pub title: String,
    #[ignite(form_type = "textarea", hidden_on_index)]
    pub body: String,
    pub published: bool,
    #[ignite(label = "Published on")]
    pub published_on: Option&lt;chrono::NaiveDate&gt;,
}</code></pre>
  <p>The struct attributes are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>table</code>, the Diesel table module. Required.</li>
    <li><code>title</code>, the heading. Defaults to the table name, capitalized.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The field attributes are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>primary_key</code>, for a key that is not named <code>id</code>. The key must be an <code>i32</code> or an <code>i64</code>. It is left out of the form.</li>
    <li><code>label</code>, the label on the form and the column heading. Defaults to the field name, capitalized, with underscores as spaces.</li>
    <li><code>form_type</code>, one of <code>text</code>, <code>textarea</code>, <code>password</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>checkbox</code> or <code>date</code>. Defaults from the type: text for <code>String</code>, number for <code>i32</code> and <code>i64</code>, checkbox for <code>bool</code>, and date for <code>chrono::NaiveDate</code>.</li>
    <li><code>required</code>, or <code>required = false</code>. A field that is not an <code>Option</code> is required by default, and a <code>bool</code> is never blank.</li>
    <li><code>searchable</code> and <code>sortable</code>, for the search box and the column headings. See <a href="/docs/tables">Tables</a>.</li>
    <li><code>hidden_on_index</code>, which leaves the column out of the list. The list shows every other field, including the <code>id</code>, unless you hide it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The supported field types are <code>String</code>, <code>i32</code>, <code>i64</code>, <code>bool</code> and <code>chrono::NaiveDate</code>, and an <code>Option</code> of any of them. The derive also writes an input struct named after the model with <code>Input</code> added, here <code>ArticleInput</code>, which holds the validated form values.</p>
  <p>The derive writes the whole trait impl, so it has three limits. It writes no dashboard cards, so a resource that needs them is written by hand. It does not offer the <code>select</code> field kind. And it does not hash a <code>password</code> field: the value is stored as the form sends it.</p>

  <h2>Write a resource by hand</h2>
  <p>A hand-written resource implements the trait itself. This one manages a <code>tags</code> table with a choice field, a search and a sort, and a dashboard card:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use diesel::prelude::*;
use diesel::result::QueryResult;
use diesel::sql_types::BigInt;
use ignite_core::{
    escape_like, Column, Direction, Field, FieldErrors, FieldKind, FormInput, IgniteResource,
    ListQuery, Page, StatWidget,
};
use serde::Serialize;

use crate::schema::tags;

const KINDS: &amp;[(&amp;str, &amp;str)] = &amp;[("color", "Colour"), ("topic", "Topic")];

const NAME: Field = Field {
    name: "name",
    label: "Name",
    kind: FieldKind::Text,
    required: true,
};

const KIND: Field = Field {
    name: "kind",
    label: "Kind",
    kind: FieldKind::Select(KINDS),
    required: true,
};

#[derive(Queryable, Selectable, Serialize)]
#[diesel(table_name = tags)]
pub struct TagRecord {
    pub id: i64,
    pub name: String,
    pub kind: String,
}

pub struct TagInput {
    pub name: String,
    pub kind: String,
}

pub struct Tag;

impl IgniteResource for Tag {
    type Record = TagRecord;
    type Input = TagInput;

    const SLUG: &amp;'static str = "tags";
    const TITLE: &amp;'static str = "Tags";

    fn widgets(conn: &amp;mut MysqlConnection) -&gt; QueryResult&lt;Vec&lt;StatWidget&gt;&gt; {
        let total: i64 = tags::table.count().get_result(conn)?;
        Ok(vec![StatWidget {
            label: "Tags",
            value: total,
        }])
    }

    fn fields() -&gt; &amp;'static [Field] {
        &amp;[NAME, KIND]
    }

    fn columns() -&gt; &amp;'static [Column] {
        &amp;[
            Column {
                key: "name",
                label: "Name",
                sortable: true,
                searchable: true,
            },
            Column {
                key: "kind",
                label: "Kind",
                sortable: false,
                searchable: false,
            },
        ]
    }

    fn list(conn: &amp;mut MysqlConnection, query: &amp;ListQuery) -&gt; QueryResult&lt;Page&lt;TagRecord&gt;&gt; {
        let pattern = query
            .search
            .as_ref()
            .map(|text| format!("%{}%", escape_like(text)));
        let mut rows = tags::table.into_boxed();
        let mut count = tags::table.into_boxed();
        if let Some(pattern) = &amp;pattern {
            rows = rows.filter(tags::name.like(pattern.clone()));
            count = count.filter(tags::name.like(pattern.clone()));
        }
        rows = match &amp;query.sort {
            Some((key, Direction::Asc)) if key == "name" =&gt; rows.order(tags::name.asc()),
            Some((key, Direction::Desc)) if key == "name" =&gt; rows.order(tags::name.desc()),
            _ =&gt; rows.order(tags::id.asc()),
        };

        let total: i64 = count.count().get_result(conn)?;
        let rows = rows
            .limit(query.per_page)
            .offset(query.offset())
            .select(TagRecord::as_select())
            .load(conn)?;
        Ok(Page { rows, total })
    }

    fn find(conn: &amp;mut MysqlConnection, id: i64) -&gt; QueryResult&lt;Option&lt;TagRecord&gt;&gt; {
        tags::table
            .find(id)
            .select(TagRecord::as_select())
            .first(conn)
            .optional()
    }

    fn create(conn: &amp;mut MysqlConnection, input: TagInput) -&gt; QueryResult&lt;TagRecord&gt; {
        diesel::insert_into(tags::table)
            .values((tags::name.eq(&amp;input.name), tags::kind.eq(&amp;input.kind)))
            .execute(conn)?;
        let id: i64 = diesel::select(diesel::dsl::sql::&lt;BigInt&gt;("LAST_INSERT_ID()"))
            .get_result(conn)?;
        Self::find(conn, id)?.ok_or(diesel::result::Error::NotFound)
    }

    fn update(conn: &amp;mut MysqlConnection, id: i64, input: TagInput) -&gt; QueryResult&lt;TagRecord&gt; {
        diesel::update(tags::table.find(id))
            .set((tags::name.eq(&amp;input.name), tags::kind.eq(&amp;input.kind)))
            .execute(conn)?;
        Self::find(conn, id)?.ok_or(diesel::result::Error::NotFound)
    }

    fn delete(conn: &amp;mut MysqlConnection, id: i64) -&gt; QueryResult&lt;usize&gt; {
        diesel::delete(tags::table.find(id)).execute(conn)
    }

    fn validate(form: &amp;FormInput) -&gt; Result&lt;TagInput, FieldErrors&gt; {
        let mut errors = FieldErrors::new();
        let name = NAME.check_required(form, &amp;mut errors);
        let kind = KIND.check_required(form, &amp;mut errors);
        let (Some(name), Some(kind)) = (name, kind) else {
            return Err(errors);
        };
        Ok(TagInput {
            name: name.to_string(),
            kind: kind.to_string(),
        })
    }
}</code></pre>
  <p>The methods are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>fields</code> and <code>columns</code>, the form fields and the list columns, in display order. A column's key matches a field name.</li>
    <li><code>list</code>, which returns one page of rows for a <code>ListQuery</code>, with the total count. It must apply the search and the sort the query asks for, and it should sort by the primary key when no sort is given.</li>
    <li><code>find</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code> and <code>delete</code>, which work on one row. <code>create</code> and <code>update</code> take the validated <code>Input</code> and return the row as the <code>Record</code>.</li>
    <li><code>validate</code>, which turns the submitted form into the <code>Input</code>, or returns an error message for each field. Call <code>check_required</code> for each required field.</li>
    <li><code>widgets</code>, the dashboard cards. See <a href="/docs/widgets">Widgets</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The <code>Record</code> is what the database returns. It must serialize to JSON with an <code>id</code> key holding the primary key as a number, and with keys named after the fields and columns. The panel builds the row links from <code>id</code>.</p>

  <h2>Add it to the panel</h2>
  <p>Pass each resource type to the panel builder, as <a href="/docs/install">Installation</a> shows: <code>.resource::&lt;Article&gt;()</code> and <code>.resource::&lt;Tag&gt;()</code>.</p>
@endsection
